d_jaracz@oz.plymouth.edu (David R. Jaracz) writes:
>I went to eat a new tin I found, hoping for some spinach.  Now, I know
>you can die from overeating, so when it wasn't spinach I decided not
>to eat it.  I hit the wrong key and started to eat it.  It asked me:
>"Keep eating?  [y/n]"  OK, here's the bug:  I accidently hit space
>bar, and it went to the "default setting".  Unfortunately, the
>"default" for this situation is keep eating, which killed me.  If you
>hit a wrong key and it goes to a default, the default should be
>beneficial to the player, not life threatening.  The default should be
>stop eating.
>
	I heartily agree.  The space key on my terminal occasionally sends
two spaces with just one push, and I've lost two high-level characters because
I unintentionally continued to eat until I burst at the seams...  8^P~~
	Personally, I wouldn't mind seeing this aspect of the game removed
entirely.  It just isn't realistic.  IMHO when you can't possibly eat any more
the correct message to give should be something like, "You can't possibly eat
another bite. --more-- You stop eating the food."
	Ideally the default in any case should be the first letter listed as
a choice.  It should also give the safety benefit of the doubt to the player.
So "Keep eating?  [y/n]" should be "Keep eatin?  [n/y]".  IMHO, of course.
